  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 245 PM CDT Thu Aug 13 2026\n\nCurrently, broad upper level ridging across the central CONUS\nwith much of the Northern Plains in the right entrance region of\na strong upper jet streak located north of the Great Lakes \nregion and over the New England area. Overrunning flow across \nwestern North Dakota coupled with embedded mid level impulses \nriding over the aforementioned ridge contributing to scattered \nshowers this afternoon west into north central. Only weak MUCAPE\nabout, roughly a few hundred J/kg, but enough for a few \nisolated showers now popping up over central ND where cloud \ncover decreased earlier allowing for some sfc heating.\nInstability and shear remain weak through the day, so while a \nfew thunderstorms are forecast, the threat for severe weather is\nvery low if not zero. \n\nWe remain on track for numerous showers and a few thunderstorms\ndeveloping tonight into tomorrow over western into north central\nNorth Dakota, with the greatest probability for heavier rainfall\nover the far northwest. This materializes as a surface trough,\nnow developing over southeastern Montana, develops over eastern\nMT into western ND late tonight/Friday AM, coupled with a well-\ndefined embedded S/WV lifting northeast across eastern MT to\nacross northwest North Dakota 06Z tonight through Friday morning\n12-18Z. Any heavier shower/thunderstorm will be capable of heavy\nrainfall given elevated PW values, though models in pretty good\nagreement that the best combined forcing will be over northwest\nNorth Dakota, so maybe pockets of heavier precip outside of this\narea. EFI values remain focused northwest as well, now at 90\npercent with shift of tails still at a value of 2 (all pointing\nto an extreme QPF event). We opted to maintain no hydro \nheadlines for this potential event, as a best practice is to \ntreat these as short-fused products once it becomes more evident\nthat elevated messaging is needed. Will continue to highlight \nin the HWO. \n\nAforementioned sfc trough advances east across western and\ncentral North Dakota during the day Friday along with a\ncontinued unsettled southwest flow aloft with multiple embedded\nimpulses pushing through. This will bring decent chances for \nshowers and a few thunderstorms to most areas Friday morning and\nafternoon, ending gradually Friday night as forcing decreases\nand high pressure builds in from the west. Locally heavy precip\nremains possible with PW values remaining elevated until late\nFriday night.\n\nThe remainder of the weekend will be mainly dry and cooler, \nwith surface high pressure over the region along with zonal flow\naloft. Embedded waves riding over a broad upper ridge building\nover the Rockies look to bring some chances for\nshowers/thunderstorms to the local region during the day Sunday\ninto Sunday night.  \n\nA quasi-zonal flow aloft is advertised for the first half of\nnext week, and will result in temperatures trending warmer/more\nseasonal, along with daily chances for showers and\nthunderstorms.",
